

What you may see if you ask for a silly high percentage of
heads in the results. Also yeah yeah I did compile with a
stack of GCC compiler flags that disables all optimizations
and also all inline internal "builtin" magic that GCC will
often do. 

This is a silly way to compile for x86_64 :
oberon$ /opt/bw/gcc14/bin/gcc -std=iso9899:1999 -pedantic \
-pedantic-errors -m64 -g -O0 -fno-builtin -march=k8 -mtune=k8 \
-mieee-fp -mhard-float -fno-fast-math -mno-fancy-math-387 \
-malign-double -m128bit-long-double -mstackrealign \
-mpreferred-stack-boundary=8 -ffp-contract=off -fno-inline \
-fno-early-inlining -fmath-errno \
-fno-unsafe-math-optimizations -o coin_flip coin_flip.c 


For the sake of something silly we can look at an old Sun SPARCv9
server : 

sphinx$ 
sphinx$ uname -a 
Linux sphinx 5.15.88-gentoo-sparc64 #1 Sun Mar 12 18:36:19 GMT 2023 sparc64 sun4u TI UltraSparc IIi (Sabre) GNU/Linux
sphinx$ 
sphinx$ which gcc
/usr/bin/gcc
sphinx$ 
sphinx$ gcc --version
gcc (Gentoo 13.2.1_p20240210 p14) 13.2.1 20240210
Copyright (C) 2023 Free Software Foundation, Inc.
This is free software; see the source for copying conditions.  There is NO
warranty; not even for MERCHANTABILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE.

sphinx$ 
sphinx$ gcc -std=iso9899:1999 -pedantic -pedantic-errors -m64 -g -O0 \
> -fno-builtin -fno-fast-math -mhard-float -mno-app-regs -mflat \
> -msoft-quad-float -mno-unaligned-doubles -mno-faster-structs \
> -mcpu=ultrasparc -mtune=ultrasparc -mno-vis -mno-cbcond \
> -mno-fmaf -mno-fsmuld -mno-popc -mno-subxc -mcmodel=medany \
> -mmemory-model=tso -fPIC -Wl,-rpath=/usr/lib64 \
> -Wl,-rpath=/usr/lib64 -Wl,-pie \
> -D_LARGEFILE64_SOURCE -D_XOPEN_SOURCE=600 -o coin_flip coin_flip.c 
sphinx$ 

